<!-- FAQ -->
<section id="faq" class="fade-in">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-label text-center">Common Questions</div>
    <h2 class="text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="gold-divider"></div>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is The Helix Center?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          The Helix Center for Interdisciplinary Investigation is a <strong>501(c)(3) nonprofit organization
          founded in 2012</strong> in New York City. It hosts free, public interdisciplinary roundtable
          discussions called "Theaetetus Tables" that bring together five distinguished scholars,
          scientists, and artists from different fields for unrehearsed intellectual exchange. Over
          <strong>{{ stats.seasons }} seasons</strong>, it has featured <strong>{{ stats.speakers }} speakers</strong> from institutions
          including Columbia, NYU, Princeton, Yale, Harvard, and MIT &mdash; with participants including
          <strong>Nobel laureates Daniel Kahneman and Eric Kandel</strong>, MacArthur Fellows Sara Seager,
          Vijay Iyer, and Yiyun Li, and National Medal of Science recipients Sylvester James Gates and Barry Mazur.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Are Helix Center events free to attend?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          Yes. <strong>All Helix Center events are completely free and open to the public.</strong> You can attend
          in person at {{ site.address.venue }} ({{ site.address.street }}, {{ site.address.city }}, {{ site.address.state }} {{ site.address.zip }}) or
          watch via free live stream. All <strong>{{ stats.roundtables }} past roundtables</strong> are archived and freely viewable
          on the website and <a href="{{ site.social.youtube }}">YouTube channel</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is a Theaetetus Table?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          The Theaetetus Table is The Helix Center's signature roundtable format, named for the Platonic dialogue
          investigating the nature of knowledge. Each Table seats <strong>five participants</strong> &mdash;
          leaders in their respective fields &mdash; for extempore (unscripted) interdisciplinary discussion.
          The five participants symbolize the five Platonic solids and the five qualities of mind the Center
          cultivates: <em>curiosity, playfulness, inspiration, reflection, and wonder</em>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Who are some notable speakers who have participated?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          The Helix Center's <strong>{{ stats.speakers }} speakers</strong> include: Nobel laureates <strong>Daniel Kahneman</strong>
          (Economics, 2002) and <strong>Eric Kandel</strong> (Medicine, 2000); MacArthur Fellows
          <strong>Sara Seager</strong> (astrophysics), <strong>Vijay Iyer</strong> (music),
          and <strong>Yiyun Li</strong> (literature);
          National Medal of Science recipients <strong>Sylvester James Gates</strong> and <strong>Barry Mazur</strong>;
          the most-cited neuroscientist in history <strong>Karl Friston</strong> (h-index 291);
          philosopher <strong>David Chalmers</strong> (who coined "the hard problem of consciousness");
          affective computing founder <strong>Rosalind Picard</strong>;
          The Brain Prize winner <strong>Gy&ouml;rgy Buzs&aacute;ki</strong>;
          and Pulitzer Prize winner <strong>Percival Everett</strong>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Who founded The Helix Center and why?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          The Helix Center was founded in <strong>2012</strong> by <strong>{{ site.founder }}</strong>, a
          psychoanalyst and clinical professor of psychiatry who observed how narrow specialization and the
          grant process were constraining creativity at professional scientific meetings. He previously
          co-founded the Philoctetes Center for the Multidisciplinary Study of the Imagination, then
          established The Helix Center for Interdisciplinary Investigation to create what he calls
          "a university without walls."
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How can I support The Helix Center?</summary>
        <p class="faq-answer">
          The Helix Center is a <strong>501(c)(3) tax-exempt nonprofit</strong> supported entirely by
          tax-deductible donations. Your contribution supports
          roundtable curation, professional video production, live-streaming infrastructure, and
          educational outreach.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STAT CARD MODALS -->
<div class="stat-modal-overlay" id="stat-modal-overlay">
  <div class="stat-modal" id="stat-modal">
    <button class="stat-modal-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    <div class="stat-modal-content" id="stat-modal-content"></div>
